<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Louis II, Duke of Bourbon</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1337">1337</a>–<a href="/years/1410">1410</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as Louis the Good · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-bourbon">House of Bourbon</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Louis II, Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-bourbon">Bourbon</a>, called the Good Duke, was a French prince who fought in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hundred-years-war">Hundred Years' War</a> and led a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">crusade</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahdia">Mahdia</a> in 1390. His marriage to Anne, heiress of Forez, and his inheritance of Beaujolais greatly enlarged the Bourbon lands, which became one of the largest princely territories in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/louis-ii-duke-of-bourbon.jpg" alt="Louis II, Duke of Bourbon" width="320" height="704" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Louis_II_de_Bourbon.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Louis de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-bourbon">Bourbon</a>, called the Good, was the third Duke of Bourbon and a prince of the blood, also Count of Clermont-en-Beauvaisis, Lord of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beaujeu">Beaujeu</a> and Grand Chamberman of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> from 1357 to 1410. He was the son of Peter I, Duke of Bourbon, and Isabella of Valois, a sister of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-vi-of-france">Philip VI of France</a>. He inherited the duchy when his father was killed at the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poitiers">Poitiers</a> in 1356, when King John II was captured by the English. In 1360 he went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> as one of the high-ranking hostages for John's ransom and was released in 1367.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Under King Charles V Louis led the royal armies in Poitou and Guyenne to recover lands lost to the English after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-bretigny">Treaty of Brétigny</a>. Following the advice of Constable Bertrand du Guesclin, he avoided large battles until the French had gained an advantage, and took many strongholds by skirmishing. In 1371 he captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/limoges">Limoges</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-duke-of-berry">John, Duke of Berry</a>, and in 1374 he took strongholds in Rouergue, Poitou and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/touraine">Touraine</a> and recaptured Bigorre and La Réole with Louis I, Duke of Anjou. He also fought the routiers who ravaged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and was sent to Normandy against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-ii-of-navarre">Charles II of Navarre</a>.</p><p>After Charles V's death in 1380 Louis was one of the regents for the young Charles VI until the king dismissed him in 1388. He fought the English in Guyenne until the Truce of Leulinghem in 1389. In 1390 he launched the Barbary Crusade against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hafsid-dynasty">Hafsids</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunis">Tunis</a>, together with the Genoese, to suppress piracy based at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahdia">Mahdia</a>; the siege failed. He married Anne of Auvergne, Countess of Forez, on 19 August 1371.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>After Charles VI's mental illness began in 1392, Louis played an important part in politics. The princes of the blood competed for the treasury and power, and Louis supported his nephew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-i-duke-of-orleans">Louis I, Duke of Orléans</a>, against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-the-bold">Philip the Bold</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a> and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-the-fearless">John the Fearless</a>, who had the Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orleans">Orléans</a> murdered in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> in 1407. Louis retreated to his estates. His son John of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-bourbon">Bourbon</a> later became a leader of the Armagnac faction. In 1410 envoys of Charles I, Duke of Orléans, invited him to join the Armagnac-Burgundian conflict; he refused, saying that such wars were quickly begun but slow to heal. He died at Montluçon on 10 August 1410, aged 73, and was succeeded by John, later captured at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-agincourt">Agincourt</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Louis II, Duke of Bourbon” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1390 a French and Genoese expedition under <b>Louis II, Duke of Bourbon</b>, besieged Mahdia without success.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hafsid-sultanate">Hafsid Sultanate · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1371 the heiress Anne of Forez married <b>Louis II, Duke of Bourbon</b>, and the county joined the Duchy of Bourbon.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-forez">County of Forez</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Louis II</b>, called the Good Duke, was a model crusading prince.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-bourbon">Duchy of Bourbon</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1400 the last lord, Edward II, childless and in debt, left the Beaujolais to <b>Louis II, Duke of Bourbon</b>, and it joined the Duchy of Bourbon.</blockquote><a href="/state/lordship-of-beaujeu">Lordship of Beaujeu</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in Montluçon, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=46.34,2.6&amp;year=1384" aria-label="Montluçon on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the place of death of Louis II, Duke of Bourbon"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5154 6154h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5154 6154h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=46.34,2.6&amp;year=1384">What was here in 1384? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
